    {
      "@id": "references/9789240081949-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Classification of digital interventions, services and applications in health: a shared language to describe the uses of digital technology for health",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2023-10-24",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240081949 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/373581",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This second edition of the Classification of Digital Interventions, Services and Applications in Health provides a standardized taxonomy for describing how digital technologies are used to address individual and health system needs. Developed by WHO, the classification establishes a shared language to support communication among policy-makers, health programme managers, researchers, implementers, technology developers and other stakeholders involved in digital health. It organizes digital health interventions around discrete technological capabilities designed to address health system challenges and links these interventions to the digital services and application types through which they are delivered.\n\nThe publication updates and expands the first edition published in 2018 to reflect advances in digital health technologies, evolving implementation practices and lessons learned from global use. The framework is structured around three interconnected axes: health system challenges, digital health interventions and digital services and application types. It includes interventions for persons, health-care providers, health system managers and data services, alongside categories of digital applications and information systems. The classification is intended to support planning, implementation, investment analysis, research, interoperability considerations, policy development and the governance of digital health initiatives, thereby facilitating more consistent and effective use of digital technologies in health systems.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240081949-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240081949-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240085138-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital adaptation kit for HIV: operational requirements for implementing WHO recommendations in digital systems",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2023-12-21",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240085138 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/375230",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This second edition of the digital adaptation kit for HIV provides operational, software-neutral requirements to support the incorporation of WHO clinical, public health and data use recommendations into digital systems. Developed within the SMART Guidelines approach, it focuses on the content requirements for person-centred digital tracking and decision support systems used by health workers for HIV services in primary health care settings. The kit is intended to facilitate transparent, consistent and adaptable translation of narrative guidance into digital implementations while supporting interoperability and local customization.\n\nThe publication organizes HIV-related requirements into eight components: health interventions and associated recommendations; generic personas; illustrative user scenarios; business processes and workflows; core data elements; decision support and scheduling logic; indicators and performance metrics; and high-level functional and non-functional requirements. It covers processes including HIV testing, pre-exposure prophylaxis, care and treatment, prevention of mother-to-child transmission, infant diagnosis, diagnostics, client follow-up, referral, and aggregate reporting and data use, with complementary operational resources provided in web annexes.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240085138-eng.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240081291-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "National health workforce accounts: a handbook",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2023-11-23",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240081291 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/374320",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA) Handbook, second edition, provides the conceptual framework, standards, methods and indicators that support the systematic collection, analysis and use of health workforce data for evidence-informed policy-making and planning. Developed by the World Health Organization, the handbook supports implementation of the National Health Workforce Accounts as a country-led system to strengthen human resources for health information systems, improve data quality and availability, and monitor progress towards universal health coverage, the Sustainable Development Goals and other global health objectives. It describes the rationale, principles and applications of NHWA at national, regional and global levels and outlines a modular approach to health workforce monitoring.\n\nThe handbook presents guidance on implementation and reporting methods and defines a set of standardized indicators organized into four modules covering workforce stock and flows, education, finance and expenditures, and working conditions, governance and leadership. It explains indicator metadata, calculation methods, disaggregation requirements and reporting standards, and illustrates how NHWA data can be used to support workforce planning, policy development, retention strategies, equity analyses and monitoring of health workforce trends. The revised edition incorporates updated indicators, occupational classifications, tools and guidance to address evolving health workforce priorities and emerging global health challenges.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240081291-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240081291-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240099456-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital adaptation kit for immunizations: operational requirements for implementing WHO recommendations in digital systems",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2024-12-31",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240099456 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/380303",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This publication presents the World Health Organization’s digital adaptation kit (DAK) for immunizations, developed within the SMART Guidelines framework to support the implementation of WHO immunization recommendations in digital systems. The document provides operational and software-neutral specifications intended to facilitate the translation of immunization guidance into interoperable digital health applications, particularly person-centred point-of-service systems and electronic immunization registries. It outlines the role of digital adaptation kits in strengthening digital health systems by improving the consistency, transparency and standardization of health content, workflows, data structures and decision-support logic used in immunization programmes. The publication also situates the DAK within broader digital health strategies and interoperability standards.\n\nThe document describes eight core components of the immunization DAK, including health interventions and recommendations, generic personas, user scenarios, workflows, core data elements, decision-support logic, indicators and functional requirements. It incorporates WHO guidance related to routine immunization, electronic immunization registries and digital health interventions, while emphasizing adaptability to country-specific policies and implementation contexts. Additional sections address interoperability, data standards, workflow optimization and the use of digital systems to support longitudinal tracking, reporting, decision support and communication within immunization services. The publication is intended for immunization programme managers, digital health teams, software developers and implementers involved in designing and deploying digital immunization systems.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240099456-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240099456-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240101197-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital transformation handbook for health supply chain architecture",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2024-11-11",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240101197 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/379506",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This handbook provides practical guidance for countries seeking to digitally transform public health supply chains through the development of coherent strategies, interoperable architectures and sustainable implementation approaches. Developed within the WHO SMART Guidelines framework, it addresses the growing need for integrated digital ecosystems that support forecasting, procurement, warehousing, distribution, dispensing and traceability of health products. The publication outlines the role of digital technologies in improving efficiency, data availability, interoperability and decision-making across supply chain processes, while helping ensure timely access to quality-assured medicines and health products.\n\nThe publication presents a stepwise approach for defining a digital health supply chain (DHSC) strategy, assessing existing health supply chain information systems, designing a target architecture, planning implementation and supporting product traceability. It emphasizes stakeholder engagement, alignment with national health, digital health and supply chain strategies, use of open standards, and long-term scalability and sustainability. Drawing on country experiences and established digital health frameworks, the handbook offers practical tools, templates and examples to support governance, planning and coordination of digital transformation initiatives, with the aim of strengthening supply chain performance, improving visibility across the supply chain and enhancing patient safety.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240101197-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240093362-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital transformation handbook for primary health care: optimizing person-centred point of service systems",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2024-11-04",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240093362 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/379452",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This handbook provides practical guidance for countries seeking to digitally transform primary health care through the development and implementation of person-centred point of service systems (PCPOSS). It addresses the transition from paper-based and fragmented digital systems towards integrated, interoperable digital solutions that support service delivery, continuity of care, data use and health system strengthening. Grounded in WHO recommendations on digital interventions for health system strengthening and aligned with the SMART Guidelines framework, the publication outlines approaches for translating clinical and public health guidance into digital systems while maintaining fidelity to normative standards.\n\nThe publication presents a stepwise methodology covering assessment of digital readiness, identification of user requirements, workflow and business process mapping, system design and adaptation, software selection, testing, deployment and scale-up. It emphasizes person-centred care, interoperability, longitudinal health records, decision support and the use of standards-based digital architectures. Through practical tools, worksheets and implementation guidance, the publication supports policy-makers, programme managers, digital health practitioners and software developers in designing and optimizing digital systems that improve service quality, strengthen accountability and enable more effective, integrated and responsive primary health care services.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240093362-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240116191-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital transformation handbook for health product catalogue",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2025-10-30",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/383309",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This handbook provides guidance for the implementation of standardized product master data management through national product catalogues (NPCs) to support digital transformation in health supply chains. Developed within the framework of the WHO Digital Implementation Investment Guide and aligned with the SMART Guidelines initiative, the publication outlines regulatory, governance, technological and operational approaches for establishing interoperable product registries and harmonized product information systems. It explains the importance of standardized product master data for ensuring accurate identification, tracking and management of health products across manufacturers, regulatory authorities, procurement entities, warehouses, health facilities and other supply chain stakeholders.\n\nThe handbook describes policies and procedures for data governance, coding structures, interoperability standards, change management and capacity-building to support sustainable implementation. It also presents guidance on integrating standardized product master data with digital health, regulatory and supply chain systems to improve forecasting, procurement, inventory management, pharmacovigilance, product recalls and traceability. In addition, the publication highlights mechanisms for sharing product master data as a global good and leveraging WHO-hosted tools and open standards to strengthen coordination, data quality and supply chain efficiency across countries and organizations.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240116191-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240116191-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240124233-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Consolidated HIV guidelines: service delivery",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2026-07-22",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240124233 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386765",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "HIV remains a major public health challenge, particularly in low- and middle-income countries, despite the availability of effective treatment and prevention tools. Many evidence-based interventions are not implemented consistently or delivered in a timely manner, contributing to ongoing HIV transmission, advanced disease and preventable mortality. These consolidated WHO guidelines provide evidence-based recommendations to strengthen HIV service delivery through people-centred, integrated and equitable approaches that improve access to care, continuity of treatment and accelerate progress towards ending AIDS as a public health threat.\n\nThe publication consolidates all WHO recommendations on HIV service delivery issued between 2013 and 2025. It is intended to support the development of national and subnational policies, clinical guidelines and implementation guidance. It addresses key components of HIV service delivery, including linkage to care and antiretroviral therapy initiation, clinical follow-up and medication refills, adherence support and re-engagement in care, services for adolescents and young people, task sharing, decentralized service delivery, and integration with maternal and child health, tuberculosis, sexual and reproductive health, diabetes, hypertension and mental health services. Recommendations are classified as new, updated or revalidated according to WHO guideline development procedures.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240124233-eng.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240120747-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Digital transformation handbook for reporting substandard and falsified medical products",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2026-06-09",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240120747 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386358",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This handbook provides practical guidance to support countries in planning, designing and implementing digital solutions for reporting substandard and falsified medical products. These products pose a significant threat to public health, contributing to therapeutic failure, adverse effects, loss of trust in health systems and broader socioeconomic impacts. Effective detection and reporting are essential to enable regulatory authorities to investigate suspected cases, remove harmful products from circulation and contribute to global surveillance efforts through mechanisms such as WHO’s Global Surveillance and Monitoring System.\n\nThe document outlines a structured approach to digital transformation, covering assessment of the current environment, stakeholder engagement, governance and policy alignment, definition of system requirements, implementation planning, and monitoring and evaluation. It emphasizes the transition from fragmented or paper-based reporting systems to interoperable digital platforms that improve data quality, standardization and timeliness. Drawing on global experience and existing WHO frameworks, the handbook promotes the use of user-centred design, open standards and integration with pharmacovigilance systems. It is intended for ministries of health, national regulatory authorities and partners seeking to strengthen reporting systems and enhance the capacity to detect and respond to substandard and falsified medical products.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240120747-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240122925-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "National health workforce accounts: health workforce levels and trends 2026",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2026-06-22",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240122925 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386519",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This report presents the 2026 edition of the National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA) series, providing an overview of the status of NHWA implementation and an analysis of global and regional health workforce levels and trends based on official data reported by countries to the World Health Organization. Developed to support evidence-informed health workforce policy-making and planning, the report examines progress in strengthening national health workforce information systems, data availability and reporting across countries, and the use of standardized indicators to monitor human resources for health in support of universal health coverage, the Sustainable Development Goals and other health objectives.\n\nDrawing on the 2025 NHWA data release, the report analyses the distribution, density and composition of the health and care workforce across regions and income groups, highlighting persistent disparities in workforce availability. It also examines trends in workforce growth since 2006 and presents a thematic analysis of population ageing and health workforce ageing, assessing their implications for future workforce needs. The findings demonstrate substantial improvements in the availability and quality of health workforce data through NHWA implementation while underscoring continuing challenges related to workforce shortages, inequities in distribution and the need for sustained investment in health workforce planning and monitoring.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789240122925-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789240122925-eng"
    },
